#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480 :question/id #uuid "f2105fec-c85b-5928-92e6-95ed80f1326c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n${4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/math-academy-id 1414 :topic/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/math-academy-id 4026 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529332 :question-generator/id #uuid "6a33e997-8493-5804-97b4-8e2f4474cbad" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186073776.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18f1824-3dc6-5be0-ac2f-8dc7d149ea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070311} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a5f92e-26c3